Mirror of the change already shipped in the public repo (v0.3), so the
baselines this fork is pinned to describe the runtime users actually get.

gb_platform_render_lcd_off_frame() preferred g_last_guest_framebuffer once any
frame had been presented, so a guest that switched the LCD off left the
previous picture frozen on screen for the whole period. In Ghost Babel that
is ~294 ms per occurrence, at frames 90, 474, 1694 and 2034 of a scripted
run: it reads as a hitch, not as a transition.

With LCDC bit 7 clear the DMG panel loses its bias and shows a uniform blank
shade, which ensure_lcd_off_framebuffer() already builds. Render that.

The private fork was 8 lines behind the public engine on this one function,
so the private project was validating a slightly different runtime from the
one it exists to validate. Timing is unchanged: p50 16.742 ms before and
after, p95 16.935 ms, 6.5% of the frame budget spent producing a frame.
