From 90d4294638b7871fb56332faa3329c4b9f17d717 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moises Santana <191926848+msantana-01@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:26:14 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 15/36] platform: panel effects for the presented image, one
 key to cycle them

The guest reaches the screen through a single SDL_RenderCopy, so a mask drawn
with the same destination rect lands on the guest pixels without any coordinate
arithmetic. The mask is a multiply: white leaves the image alone, grey darkens
it, so one percentage controls every effect.

The hard part was making one setting hold at both ends of the range, which is
where three attempts died in a row and each failure is worth recording:

  * A mask on a fixed guest multiple snapped the gap to whole sub-columns. At
    3x a 0.22 guest-pixel gap is barely one screen pixel, and rounding it to a
    third of one turned the LCD grid into a dot matrix.
  * Rasterising at the window scale fixed the snapping but exposed the real
    issue: one screen pixel cannot be antialiased into anything visible. The
    ramp consumed the whole band, no plateau survived, and peak coverage
    collapsed to 3%. The grid did not look weak - it was not there.
  * Widening the ramp to fill the lattice made it worse, because a ramp
    centred on an edge bleeds past it and two bleeding edges turn a 0.22 gap
    into 0.4.

What works: rasterise at the on-screen scale, hold the gap to two screen pixels
rather than one, and cover each band with a trapezoid that stops at its edge
instead of bleeding. The grid draws half a gap on each side so neighbouring
borders meet. Measured at an 800x720 window, each guest pixel reads
dark-light-light-light-dark, which is the cell a DMG screen actually shows.

Guest frame dumps at frames 400, 700, 1000, 1100 and 1200 are byte-identical to
a build from before any of this, so every differential and oracle comparison
still means what it meant. ctest 84/86; the two crystal_* failures reproduce on
a clean tree. The screen effect test asserts the property that actually
matters: at every lattice the runtime can pick, the effect reaches full
strength rather than quietly rendering nothing.
