From: Moises Santana <191926848+msantana-01@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 11:57:14 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 16/36] platform: persist effect strength, and give the panel a
 response time

The strength selector was already written to and read from the prefs file, but
nothing ever called save from the slider: the value only reached disk when F9
or the combo changed the *effect*, never when the user changed the strength. So
the setting looked like it saved and did not. The fix is not just the missing
call - SliderInt returns true on every frame of a drag, which would rewrite the
file sixty times a second. Persist on release instead.

LCD Response models a panel that lags the light it emits: one exponential step
per guest frame, coefficient derived from elapsed wall time rather than from a
per-frame constant. That derivation is the entire point - a fixed coefficient
halves the trail at double the frame rate and vanishes on a fast display - and
the test asserts that one 32 ms step and four 8 ms steps land in the same place.
Darkening runs faster than lightening, which is what leaves a dark smear behind
a moving sprite instead of a symmetric blur. Off by default and persisted, so
the trail costs nothing to someone who never asked for it and survives a
restart once they do.

The step is applied to the emitted colour inside the locked texture, after the
palette, because that is what a panel lags; the guest frame is rewritten before
each step, so the target is always fresh. Once per guest frame only, which
means a repeated present never decays the trail and fast forward stretches it in
wall-clock time the way the hardware would. Loading a savestate drops the
trail: it belongs to the state that produced it, and carrying it across would
smear one scene's motion into another.

Two things the tests caught that inspection would not have. Relaxing a
one-level difference never closes under round-to-nearest, because the step is
smaller than the rounding - the panel settled one shade below the guest colour
permanently, a brightness bias invisible in any single frame and inherited by
every frame after it. Snapping once the remainder is under a level fixes it. And
a comment claiming alpha 0 was the identity was wrong: alpha 0 freezes the
panel on the previous frame, and the feature is free because the caller skips
the call, not because of any coefficient. Both now say what is true.

F4 toggles it. ctest 85/87; the two crystal_* failures reproduce on a clean
tree. Guest frame dumps at 400, 700, 1000, 1100 and 1200 are byte-identical to a
build from before any of this.
