From abf7b195fb286f01d460846860f279afb32d05da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moises Santana <191926848+msantana-01@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:30:39 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 17/36] platform: CRT as scanlines plus phosphor glow, no
 curvature

A handheld screen is not a tube, so this stops where the tube simulation would
start: scanlines and a phosphor bleed, with no barrel warp and no vignette. The
user's reason for that is also the better one - a vignette on a handheld panel
imitates a piece of geometry that was never there.

The glow cannot go in the multiply mask. A halo is brighter than the pixel that
produced it, and a multiplier can only ever darken, so it needs its own texture
composited with SDL_BLENDMODE_ADD. It is built at guest resolution and the
upscale does the spreading, the same argument as the LCD grid: one mask pixel
becoming a soft blob under linear filtering is exactly the phosphor bleed, and
the filter is forced to Linear even when the guest texture is Nearest.

Normalising the blur is the whole ballgame, and there is a trap in it. Dividing
by the sum of the kernel preserves DC gain: a flat bright region comes back at
its own brightness. Dividing by the peak of the kernel looks better on an
isolated pixel and multiplies every flat area by the square of the kernel sum -
which blew the entire screen to white, with saturated channels. The first
version here did exactly that. The test asserts the flat-region property
directly, because a test that only looks at one hot pixel will happily pass a
kernel that explodes everywhere else.

Two smaller things the numbers caught. The tent weights sum to 16, not the 20
that was written next to them; the kernel was attenuating every pass by 0.8 and
the two passes compounded it into 0.64, which is not a crash and not obviously
wrong, just a permanently fainter effect. And the first threshold sat so high
that an isolated bright pixel came out at six percent of its own excess, which
is not a glow - DC-preserving blur attenuates a lone pixel hard, so the
threshold has to leave real energy in the highlights to compensate.

Measured on the game at 800x720: at 70 percent the glow adds up to 52 and the
scanlines subtract up to 173, with zero saturated channels at any strength. The
slider has real range without a blowout at the top.

ctest 86/88; the two crystal_* failures reproduce on a clean tree. Guest frame
dumps at 400, 700, 1000, 1100 and 1200 are byte-identical to a build from
before any of the panel work.
