Subject: [PATCH 19/36] platform: make the presented surface width a runtime
 value

The guest is always 160 pixels wide and that does not change. What changes is
how wide the picture we present is: a remaster builds a tile renderer covering
more than the original viewport, and the platform had no way to be told.

gb_platform_present_framebuffer took a bare pointer and every buffer, texture
and scaling term below it was hardcoded to GB_SCREEN_WIDTH. A wider surface
either could not be presented or could only be presented by lying about its
width.

The width now defaults to GB_SCREEN_WIDTH and the buffers are sized for the
maximum, so at the default each loop runs the same iterations over the same
addresses as before and the authentic path is unchanged. Setting a new width
destroys the textures that bake in a size and drops the persistence buffers
rather than reinterpreting them under a new stride: their contents are not the
previous frame any more, and forgetting costs one frame of response instead of
shearing one.
