From 83fe842eafa5a727af9204c8d6168d34347c82e6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moises Santana <moises.santana@ifba.edu.br>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 13:23:19 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 20/36] audio: a mastering chain for the remaster, measured
 rather than assumed
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three stages in the SDL callback, after volume and before the device: a DC
blocker, a compressor linked across channels, and a soft clip. Off by default
and a checkbox in the Esc menu, because this is an opinion about how the game
should sound and someone who came for the 1998 mix should hear the 1998 mix.

The compressor is linked rather than per-side on purpose. The APU pans the
channels itself, so compressing each side independently would move the stereo
image every time one side got quieter - the image is a fixed fact of the
hardware and has to survive. One envelope on the louder channel, one gain for
both.

Real-time safe by construction: no allocation, no locks, no unbounded loops,
fixed state, and the envelope coefficients are derived once per block instead of
per sample. The callback is the one place where a surprise is an audible glitch
rather than a slow leak.

tests/test_audio_mastering.cpp measures the four claims the chain makes -
offset gone, peaks rounded rather than clipped, loud material eased and quiet
material untouched, panning intact - plus that a long constant input does not
make the feedback filter diverge. Writing it found three bugs, all of the same
kind:

The soft clip was fed a Padé approximant of tanh unclamped, and that rational
form only matches tanh near zero and diverges upward beyond |y| = 3. A
saturation curve that amplifies is a distortion generator: a constant input came
out louder than it went in.

Then the whole chain was operating on raw int16 samples while comparing them
against normalised thresholds, so every sample looked like a peak. Nothing
crashed and it was not obviously wrong on a colourful screen - the same shape of
mistake this runtime already documents for RGB555 being handed to an ARGB
buffer. Units now convert once, at the edges.

Then the curve was replaced by one that reaches its asymptote quickly, which
made it exceed its own input just below that asymptote: 30000 measured 31708.
The shape now used has derivative at most 1 at the knee, falling to zero at the
ceiling, so touching the signal without expanding it and staying bounded are
properties of the formula rather than of a guard.

Two judgement calls worth recording. The soft clip sits below full scale rather
than at it, because a clipper mapping 1.0 to 1.0 is an identity at the one value
it exists to catch. And the DC test asserts on the settled tail rather than the
block mean: the filter's time constant is about 2000 samples, and the mean of a
decaying step response is dominated by the transient rather than by how the
filter ends up - which is how a correctly working filter first measured 488
instead of zero.

Pre-existing failures in ctest, unchanged by this: crystal_release_package_is_
deterministic_and_relocatable, crystal_standalone_contract_is_versioned_and_
fail_closed, screenshot_prefix_preserves_long_evidence_path (segfaults). All
three fail on the tree before this commit too.
