From 196741c73106aded978122364a8475f6378caac4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moises Santana <moises.santana@ifba.edu.br>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 01:46:28 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 21/36] platform: the audio valve shortens the frame wait
 instead of dropping it

When the ring fell below its low watermark the vsync served no wait at all, and
the performance document recorded what that costs: a bang-bang controller around
a threshold, with the cadence as its victim. The valve now scales the wait by how
full the ring is relative to the watermark - an empty ring runs free, a ring at
the watermark gets a full frame, which is the 60 fps the APU's rate is matched
to - so the equilibrium is the watermark itself rather than the ring's ceiling.

The other half is what the schedule does with a wait that was not served. The
old code advanced next_frame_time by the whole frame however little of it it
served, so every starved frame moved the target ten milliseconds further ahead
and the ring's recovery was paid out as one stall. The schedule now advances by
the wait the frame actually served. It is never pulled forward, so a frame that
overslept keeps its debt and later frames repay it by serving a shorter wait -
that repayment is why the cadence reaches 1.0x at all.

Measured on a 7000-frame windowed run, same binary and same instrument before and
after (--perf-report, real window):

                     before                  after
  cadence (7000 fr)  0.985x                  0.986x
  frame wait         0.001 ms .. 32.333 ms   10.060 ms .. 10.867 ms
  ring               288 .. 3616 (6.5..82 ms)  2304 .. 4576 (52..104 ms)
  underruns          32801                   30721
  dropped            35828                   35828
  produced           44113 Hz                44081 Hz

The cumulative cadence is unchanged because its deficit is the startup burst -
the window and the CRT shader take 1.2 s and the ring drains while they are
built - and not the valve. What changes is the steady state: the wait stops
alternating between nothing and a stall, and the ring stops swinging to within
6.5 ms of empty. Underruns fall by about 6% and the dropped count is identical,
which is the check that the guest's own audio production was not touched: only
the host's waiting changed.
---
 runtime/src/platform_sdl.cpp | 42 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 38 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/runtime/src/platform_sdl.cpp b/runtime/src/platform_sdl.cpp
index 8a85f93..26962aa 100644
--- a/runtime/src/platform_sdl.cpp
+++ b/runtime/src/platform_sdl.cpp
@@ -4802,13 +4802,47 @@ void gb_platform_vsync(uint32_t frame_cycles) {
     }
 
     frame_remainder = frame_ticks_num % frame_ticks_den;
-    next_frame_time += frame_ticks;
-    uint64_t target_frame_time = next_frame_time;
 
     uint32_t audio_fill = audio_ring_fill_samples();
-    bool audio_starved = audio_output_should_run() && g_audio_started && audio_fill < g_audio_low_watermark;
+    bool audio_starved = audio_output_should_run() && g_audio_started &&
+                         g_audio_low_watermark > 0u && audio_fill < g_audio_low_watermark;
 
-    if (!audio_starved && now < target_frame_time) {
+    /*
+     * Ring-short case: shorten this frame's wait instead of dropping it.
+     *
+     * The old rule was "ring below the low watermark, do not sleep at all". It
+     * is a bang-bang controller around a threshold, and it costs more cadence
+     * than it buys audio: the free-running frame fills the ring, the next frame
+     * sleeps the banked wait, the ring drains, and the cycle repeats. Measured
+     * on a 7000-frame windowed run: the cadence climbed 0.870x to 0.986x while
+     * the per-frame wait alternated between 0.001 ms and 32.3 ms and the ring
+     * swung between 6.5 ms and 82 ms.
+     *
+     * The wait is instead scaled by how full the ring is relative to the
+     * watermark. An empty ring means run free - the guest has produced and the
+     * device has taken everything - and a ring at the watermark means a full
+     * frame, which is the 60 fps the APU's rate is matched to. The equilibrium
+     * becomes the watermark itself rather than the ring's ceiling.
+     *
+     * The schedule advances by the wait the frame actually served, so the
+     * skipped portion is not banked. Banking is what turned the old valve into
+     * the second half of the cycle: frames that served nothing left the target
+     * ten milliseconds further ahead each frame, and the ring's recovery was
+     * paid out as one 32 ms stall.
+     *
+     * The schedule is still never pulled forward, so a frame that overslept
+     * keeps its debt and later frames repay it by serving a shorter wait. That
+     * repayment is why the cadence reaches 1.0x at all.
+     */
+    uint64_t frame_wait_ticks = frame_ticks;
+    if (audio_starved) {
+        frame_wait_ticks = (frame_ticks * (uint64_t)audio_fill) / (uint64_t)g_audio_low_watermark;
+    }
+
+    next_frame_time += frame_wait_ticks;
+    uint64_t target_frame_time = next_frame_time;
+
+    if (now < target_frame_time) {
         for (;;) {
             uint64_t wait_ticks = target_frame_time - now;
             uint32_t wait_us = (uint32_t)((wait_ticks * 1000000) / freq);
