From 13c5a22aaad8c43034846af90ffe01119b9b000c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Moises Santana <moises.santana@ifba.edu.br>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:36:04 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 25/36] preferences: give each artifact its own identity keys

Two artifacts share one core, and the settings that carry their identity cannot be shared: a player who turns the mastering off in the remaster must not turn it off in the authentic too.

gb_platform_set_preference_scope() names the artifact. An identity key is then stored per scope (audio.mastering.remaster) and the bare name belongs to the unscoped build. The player's environment - bindings, device, volume - stays shared, because configuring the gamepad twice is a tax, not a feature.

A scoped artifact writes the whole file back when a setting changes, so the other artifact's identity key is read and written back untouched. Without that, saving in the remaster would silently rewrite the authentic's identity.
